  "text": "We are given a **hexagon** formed by **three congruent circular arcs** with radii **7**, **13**, and **15** units, and the **arcs intersect at their endpoints**, forming a **closed curve**. The **shortest side** of this hexagon is **25 units**.\n\nWe are to **find the area** of this hexagon.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Understand the structure\n\n- The hexagon is formed by three arcs: two with radii 13 and 15, and one with radius 7.\n- The arcs intersect at their endpoints, forming a **closed curve**.\n- The **shortest side** is 25, so this side is formed by **one of the arcs**.\n\nLet’s assume the hexagon has six sides. Since it is closed and made up of three arcs, the hexagon must be symmetric in some way.\n\nBut to proceed, we need to determine the **length of the arc** that corresponds to the side of length 25.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Use the length of the arc to find the radius\n\nLet’s suppose that the side of length 25 is formed by an arc of **radius 13** or **radius 15**, or **radius 7**.\n\nLet’s suppose the side is formed by an arc of **radius 13**.\n\nThe length of an arc is given by:\n\n$$\n\\text{Arc length} = r \\theta\n$$\n\nWhere:\n- $ r $ is the radius,\n- $ \\theta $ is the central angle in radians.\n\nWe are told that the **shortest side is 25**.\n\nLet’s suppose the side is formed by an arc of **radius 7**.\n\nThen:\n\n$$\n\\text{Arc length} = 7 \\theta = 25 \\Rightarrow \\theta = \\frac{25}{7}\n$$\n\nWe can compute $ \\theta = \\frac{25}{7} $ radians.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Use the formula for the area of a hexagon formed by three arcs\n\nThe area of a hexagon can be calculated as:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area} = \\text{Area of one arc} + \\text{Area of two triangles} + \\text{Area of two other triangles}\n$$\n\nEach triangle formed by the center of the arc and two adjacent vertices contributes a triangle with base equal to the side of the hexagon and height equal to the distance from the center to the base.\n\nLet’s denote:\n\n- $ r_1 = 7 $, $ r_2 = 13 $, $ r_3 = 15 $\n- The side of length 25 corresponds to one arc, so we use $ r = 7 $, and the central angle $ \\theta = \\frac{25}{7} $.\n\nNow, the area of a sector with radius $ r $ and angle $ \\theta $ is:\n\n$$\n\\text{Sector area} = \\frac{1}{2} r^2 \\theta\n$$\n\nSo for one arc:\n\n$$\n\\text{Sector area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 7^2 \\cdot \\frac{25}{7} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 49 \\cdot \\frac{25}{7} = \\frac{49}{2} \\cdot \\frac{25}{7} = \\frac{25 \\cdot 7}{2} = \\frac{175}{2}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Area of two triangles (top and bottom)\n\nLet’s suppose the two triangles are formed by the center and the two endpoints of the side of length 25.\n\nEach triangle has:\n\n- Base = 25\n- Height = distance from the center to the side = radius of the arc = 7 (assuming the side is formed by an arc of radius 7)\n\nSo each triangle has area:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of one triangle} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 25 \\cdot 7 = \\frac{175}{2}\n$$\n\nThere are two such triangles:\n\n$$\n\\text{Total area from triangles} = 2 \\cdot \\frac{175}{2} = 175\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 5: Add up all the areas\n\n- Area of one sector: $ \\frac{175}{2} $\n- Area of two triangles: 175\n\nSo total area:\n\n$$\n\\frac{175}{2} + 175 = \\frac{175}{2} + \\frac{350}{2} = \\frac{525}{2} = 262.5\n$$\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{262.5} \\text{ square units}\n$$\n\nOr, as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\frac{525}{2}} \\text{ square units}\n$$",
